<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Buda is the historic town on the western, hilly bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> that in 1873 merged with Pest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obuda">Óbuda</a> to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. Its castle hill became the main royal residence of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from the 14th century and the seat of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> held Buda as the centre of a province from 1541 until the Habsburg-led siege of 1686.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buda.jpg" alt="Buda" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michel Wolgemut, Wilhelm Pleydenwurff (Text: Hartmann Schedel) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuremberg_chronicles_-_BVJA.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
